<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cavaillon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cabellio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cavaillon is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a> in Provence, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Cabellio of the Cavares and a Roman colony, where a small Roman arch survives. It belonged to the papal Comtat Venaissin until 1791 and was one of the four towns where the 'Pope's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>' were allowed to live; its 18th-century synagogue survives. It is famous for its melons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cavaillon.jpg" alt="Cavaillon" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Julien Pelissier · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_du_centre_ville_de_la_ville_de_Cavaillon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
